---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import DoodleEllipse from '@components/DoodleEllipse.astro';
import DoodleScribble from '@components/DoodleScribble.astro';
import photo from '@images/story.webp';
---

<section
    id="story"
    class="section overflow-hidden relative isolate border-t border-charcoal"
    aria-labelledby="story-title"
>
    <div
        class="story__doodle absolute -z-10"
        aria-hidden="true"
    >
        <DoodleScribble />
    </div>
    <div class="shell story__grid">
        <Image
            class="story__photo col-span-5 col-start-1"
            alt="Leslie and Yolanda"
            data-scroll="left"
            sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 466px"
            src={photo}
            width={600}
            widths={[600, 900, 1200]}
        />
        <div class="col-span-6 col-start-7">
            <p
                class="eyebrow mb-12"
                data-scroll="right"
            >
                Our story
            </p>
            <h2
                id="story-title"
                class="story__title"
                data-scroll="right"
            >
                It started on a <span class="inline-block relative whitespace-nowrap">FaceTime call.<DoodleEllipse /></span>
            </h2>
            <div
                class="story__body"
                data-scroll="right"
            >
                <p>The Figure Zero Project was founded by two high school friends, Leslie and Yolanda. Honestly, they didn't have a lot in common. Yolanda can be quite calm; Leslie, not so much. Despite this, they shared a similar passion for catching sunrises at Woodbine Beach, crying at the Eras Tour, hwachae, almost peeing their pants laughing at photo booths, and, most importantly, art.</p>
                <p>Although they eventually found themselves pursuing science, they found a way to bring art and scientific research together through the Figure Zero Project. Their journey began with illustrating journal covers, which soon grew to include a larger team that designs journal layouts and visual abstracts, and translates complex articles into words that anyone can understand. What started as an ordinary idea during a FaceTime call turned into a growing contribution to scientific communication. And they are only getting started.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<style>
    .story__body {
        color: var(--color-umber);
        font-size: 17px;
        line-height: 1.7;
        max-width: var(--measure-prose);
        text-wrap: pretty;
    }

    .story__body p:not(:last-child) {
        margin-bottom: 20px;
    }

    .story__body p:first-of-type::first-letter {
        float: left;
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-size: clamp(48px, calc(42.67px + 1.6667vw), 64px);
        line-height: 0.8;
        padding: 6px 10px 0 0;
    }

    .story__doodle {
        height: clamp(110px, calc(86.67px + 7.29vw), 180px);
        left: max(-36px, calc(50% - 680px));
        opacity: 0.08;
        top: clamp(28px, calc(9.33px + 5.83vw), 84px);
        width: clamp(180px, calc(140px + 12.5vw), 300px);
    }

    .story__grid {
        align-items: start;
        display: grid;
        gap: 28px;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    }

    .story__photo {
        display: block;
        height: auto;
        width: 100%;
    }

    .story__title {
        font-size: clamp(34px, calc(25.33px + 2.71vw), 60px);
        font-weight: 460;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        line-height: 1.05;
        margin-bottom: 48px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 1024px) {
        .story__grid {
            display: block;
        }

        .story__grid > * {
            margin-bottom: 40px;
        }

        .story__grid > :last-child {
            margin-bottom: 0;
        }
    }
</style>
